Jens Schmalzing <jens.schmalzing@physik.uni-muenchen.de> writes:

> Steve VanDevender writes:
>
>> Having actually done some software internationalization work, I would
>> have to say NetHack would be a nightmare to translate.
>
> I had that feeling without having done that kind of work.  Can you
> point to literature, starters, whatever?

There's probably formal literature on this but about twelve years ago
when i was doing this for a job I never really had a chance to look for
it.

The basic idea is:

1.  Find all messages in the program source and any associated data
files that might be displayed to the user.

2.  Translate them.

This of course can get pretty hard.  One of the most difficult portions
of a program I worked on involved a lot of message substitution; a
paragraph of text would be displayed for the user, with certain words
highlighted as clickable buttons.  Click one of the buttons and you
would get alternative words you could replace it with.  This drove our
German translator nuts; she initially wanted to make substantial
rearrangements to the structure of sentences based on the word used,
because she didn't understand that it all came from a single template in
the English version.  She felt there was no simple German template that
wouldn't look awkward somehow, but I explained it would be incredibly
difficult to program the kind of variation she initially wanted.

Another discovery was that German expands the total number of characters
displayed by as much as 50%, often making it difficult to fit the
translated text in the available screen space.

In NetHack something as simple as "The %s hits!" would involve
substantial work to translate to German.  You'd have to associate a
gender with each monster name so you could pick the right article to go
with it (and in German, this would require a large table of definite,
indefinite, possessive, etc. articles in each of the four cases).  You
also would have to have a table of some kind of information to be able
to modify each monster name for agreement with each grammatical case the
name would be used in.

Finding most the messages would involve reading the source code closely,
although at least it would not be too painful to translate the fortunes,
monster descriptions, and so on in the external data files.  And since
many of the messages are generated from printf()-style templates
involving various kinds of substitutions, you're going to be doing a lot
of detail work.  Not to mention the problems caused by the You() style
functions (the code is full of stuff like You("feel full of hot air!")).
And then there's all the infrastructure in the code just for English
that you would have to strip out or modify (there are routines for doing
things like determining whether to use "a" or "an" in front of a monster
name, creating the English plural or possessive form of a monster name,
and so on, which mainly involve heuristics and special-case tests).

At least that's the kind of thing I had to do when working on a game
program that did a lot of the same things (although it was a flight
simulator game, and far less message-intensive that NetHack).  My
manager hilariously underestimated the complexity of that project ("I
figure it will take a couple of weeks.  All we have to do is drop German
words in.")  and having never worked on this kind of thing before I was
too naive to disillusion him about this until I had waded in far enough
to discover just how difficult it would be.  It ultimately took two
months of work by me and two German translators to adapt the program.

Maintaining a translation and tracking new releases of NetHack will
probably be difficult too; you would have to diff releases and then
(re)write code for each message added or changed.

